## Feature folder structure (recommended)

```
src/
├── app/
│   ├── store.ts          # configureStore, RootState, AppDispatch exports
│   └── hooks.ts          # typed useAppSelector, useAppDispatch
├── features/
│   ├── cart/
│   │   ├── cartSlice.ts  # createSlice — reducer + action creators
│   │   ├── cartSelectors.ts  # createSelector memos
│   │   ├── cartThunks.ts     # createAsyncThunk / manual thunks
│   │   ├── Cart.tsx          # UI component
│   │   └── cart.test.ts
│   ├── user/
│   │   ├── userSlice.ts
│   │   ├── userSelectors.ts
│   │   └── userThunks.ts
│   └── products/
│       ├── productsSlice.ts
│       └── productsApi.ts  # RTK Query endpoint definitions
```

## store.ts

```ts
import { configureStore } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';
import cartReducer from '../features/cart/cartSlice';
import userReducer from '../features/user/userSlice';
import { productsApi } from '../features/products/productsApi';

export const store = configureStore({
  reducer: {
    cart: cartReducer,
    user: userReducer,
    [productsApi.reducerPath]: productsApi.reducer,
  },
  middleware: (getDefault) =>
    getDefault().concat(productsApi.middleware),
});

export type RootState  = ReturnType<typeof store.getState>;
export type AppDispatch = typeof store.dispatch;
```

## hooks.ts — typed hooks

```ts
import { useDispatch, useSelector } from 'react-redux';
import type { RootState, AppDispatch } from './store';

export const useAppDispatch = () => useDispatch<AppDispatch>();
export const useAppSelector = useSelector.withTypes<RootState>();
```

## Why not type-based (actions/, reducers/)?

A type-based structure forces you to navigate across multiple directories for every change to a single feature. Feature folders keep cohesive code together — easier for code review, easier to delete a feature entirely, and easier to extract into a separate package later.

## Mental model

| | Redux Thunk | Redux Saga |
|---|---|---|
| Mechanism | Dispatch a function | Generator yields effects |
| Async model | async/await | yield call/put/take |
| Learning curve | Low | Medium-High |
| Cancellation | Manual (AbortController) | Built-in (cancel, race) |
| Testing | Mock dispatch / getState | Mock the effects (no real I/O) |
| Action watching | Must be triggered by component | takeEvery/takeLatest watch the action stream |

## Thunk — imperative, inline

```ts
export const fetchUser = (id: string) =>
  async (dispatch: AppDispatch) => {
    dispatch(setLoading(true));
    const user = await api.getUser(id);
    dispatch(setUser(user));
    dispatch(setLoading(false));
  };
```

## Saga — declarative, action-driven

```ts
function* fetchUserSaga(action: PayloadAction<string>) {
  yield put(setLoading(true));
  const user: User = yield call(api.getUser, action.payload);
  yield put(setUser(user));
  yield put(setLoading(false));
}

function* rootSaga() {
  yield takeLatest('user/fetch', fetchUserSaga); // auto-cancels previous
}
```

## Testing

Saga tests are particularly clean because `call(api.getUser, id)` is just a description — you assert on the yielded effect objects without needing to mock the API:

```ts
const gen = fetchUserSaga({ payload: '1', type: 'user/fetch' });
assert.deepEqual(gen.next().value,  put(setLoading(true)));
assert.deepEqual(gen.next().value,  call(api.getUser, '1'));
// etc.
```

## When to choose which

Thunk + `createAsyncThunk`: single API call, simple loading/error state, team new to Redux.
Saga: polling, request cancellation, parallel requests with takeAll, wizard flows, complex retry.

