## What the decorator desugars to

```tsx
// With decorator syntax:
@connect(mapStateToProps, mapDispatchToProps)
class UserProfile extends React.Component<Props> {
  render() {
    return <div>{this.props.name}</div>;
  }
}
export default UserProfile; // already wrapped by the decorator
```

Is exactly equivalent to:

```tsx
class UserProfile extends React.Component<Props> {
  render() {
    return <div>{this.props.name}</div>;
  }
}
export default connect(mapStateToProps, mapDispatchToProps)(UserProfile);
```

The `@` syntax is syntactic sugar — the class is passed as the argument to the decorator function.

## Setting it up (legacy)

**Babel:** `@babel/plugin-proposal-decorators` with `legacy: true`.
**TypeScript:** `"experimentalDecorators": true` in tsconfig.json.

## Why it is obsolete

1. Class components themselves are now discouraged in favour of function components.
2. `useSelector` + `useDispatch` hooks replace `connect()` entirely.
3. TC39 Stage 3 decorators (2023) have a different syntax from the legacy decorator proposal, so old `@connect` code may not work with modern decorator transforms.

## Modern equivalent

```tsx
function UserProfile() {
  const name = useAppSelector((state) => state.user.name);
  return <div>{name}</div>;
}
```

No decorator, no HOC, no boilerplate.

